WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.582
Deep learning depends on huge datasets that are
costly to store, preprocess,

00:00:04.662 --> 00:00:08.743
and train on.

00:00:08.823 --> 00:00:12.905
Dataset distillation compresses a dataset into a
tiny synthetic one that still trains high-accuracy models.

00:00:12.985 --> 00:00:17.066
This paper introduces SPEED,

00:00:17.146 --> 00:00:21.228
Sparse Parameterization for Epitomic dataset Distillation.

00:00:21.308 --> 00:00:25.389
Instead of tuning the matching objective like most
prior work,

00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:29.551
SPEED rethinks how the synthetic dataset itself is
parameterized.

00:00:29.631 --> 00:00:33.712
It borrows ideas from dictionary learning and
sparse coding: a shared pool of spatial-agnostic

00:00:33.792 --> 00:00:37.874
epitomic tokens acts as a dictionary,

00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:42.035
sparse coding matrices pick the significant tokens
per image,

00:00:42.115 --> 00:00:46.197
and a feature-recurrent network reassembles them
into high-resolution synthetic images.

00:00:46.277 --> 00:00:50.358
The result is state-of-the-art distillation,

00:00:50.438 --> 00:00:54.520
especially on high-resolution ImageNet subsets,
with a fraction of the storage.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:02.303
The goal of dataset distillation is to shrink a big
dataset into a small synthetic set that still trains models well.

00:01:02.383 --> 00:01:06.397
Most existing work pours its energy into the
matching objective,

00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:09.787
the loss that aligns the synthetic and real datasets.

00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:14.968
But how the synthetic images are actually
parameterized has been an afterthought.

00:01:15.048 --> 00:01:19.253
The standard approach optimizes each synthetic
image independently,

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:26.097
a naive scheme that never exploits the fact that
natural images share enormous amounts of visual structure,

00:01:26.177 --> 00:01:29.615
both within a single image and across different images.

00:01:29.695 --> 00:01:34.220
This spatial redundancy silently wastes the already
tiny storage budget,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.610
and the problem gets worse as image resolution grows.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.324
The key insight is that images are highly redundant.

00:01:41.404 --> 00:01:46.641
Patches repeat, textures recur, and similar
structures appear across many images.

00:01:46.721 --> 00:01:51.433
Classical representation learning, dictionary
learning and sparse coding,

00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:58.260
was built exactly to capture this: represent many
signals as sparse combinations of a shared dictionary.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:04.759
A handful of recent distillation methods started
exploiting relationships between synthetic images,

00:02:04.839 --> 00:02:08.435
but none tackled redundancy in a spatially-agnostic way,

00:02:08.515 --> 00:02:10.666
no matter where a feature appears.

00:02:10.746 --> 00:02:18.084
SPEED asks: what if we spend almost none of the
storage budget on a shared dictionary and per-image sparse codes,

00:02:18.164 --> 00:02:21.892
and let a small network reconstruct rich images from them?

00:02:21.972 --> 00:02:24.780
That reframing is where the gains come from.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.488
SPEED makes three main contributions.

00:02:27.568 --> 00:02:31.002
First, it introduces spatial-agnostic epitomic tokens,

00:02:31.082 --> 00:02:35.428
a shared dictionary of tokens reused by every
synthetic image patch,

00:02:35.508 --> 00:02:41.416
together with sparse coding matrices that select
only the most significant tokens per image.

00:02:41.496 --> 00:02:44.540
Second, it proposes a feature-recurrent network,

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.657
a compact transformer-style network that
recurrently assembles those tokens into hierarchical,

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:55.864
high-resolution synthetic images while reusing the
same shared tokens and codes.

00:02:55.944 --> 00:03:01.200
Third, it shows this parameterization is a drop-in
module: it plugs into gradient,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.407
distribution, and trajectory matching objectives
alike and improves all of them.

00:03:06.487 --> 00:03:12.720
The framework sets new state-of-the-art results and
is especially strong on high-resolution data.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.012
SPEED is tested broadly.

00:03:17.092 --> 00:03:21.003
On the standard side,

00:03:21.083 --> 00:03:24.995
it uses CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 at thirty-two by
thirty-two resolution and TinyImageNet at sixty-four by sixty-four.

00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:28.986
To stress high-resolution performance,

00:03:29.066 --> 00:03:32.978
it uses six ImageNet subsets at one-twenty-eight by
one-twenty-eight,

00:03:33.058 --> 00:03:36.969
each with ten classes: ImageNette, ImageWoof,
ImageFruit, ImageMeow, ImageSquawk,

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:40.961
and ImageYellow.

00:03:41.041 --> 00:03:44.952
Robustness is measured on CIFAR-100-C with fourteen
corruption types at five severity levels.

00:03:45.032 --> 00:03:48.944
Everything is compared under equal storage budgets,
counted in parameters per class,

00:03:49.024 --> 00:03:52.935
at one, ten, and fifty images per class.

00:03:53.015 --> 00:03:56.927
The default backbone is a ConvNet,

00:03:57.007 --> 00:04:00.918
the default matching objective is trajectory matching,

00:04:00.998 --> 00:04:04.910
and generalization is checked on MLP, ResNet18, and ViT.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:09.288
The headline is a clean sweep.

00:04:09.368 --> 00:04:13.367
Across all three standard benchmarks and all six
high-resolution ImageNet subsets,

00:04:13.447 --> 00:04:17.445
SPEED sets new state-of-the-art.

00:04:17.525 --> 00:04:21.523
At the tightest one-image-per-class budget, it
reaches forty percent on CIFAR-100,

00:04:21.603 --> 00:04:25.602
a six point gain, and twenty-six point nine percent
on TinyImageNet,

00:04:25.682 --> 00:04:29.680
a ten point nine gain over the previous best.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.758
On the ImageNet subsets it averages an eleven point
two percent improvement at the same budget.

00:04:33.838 --> 00:04:37.837
Strikingly,

00:04:37.917 --> 00:04:41.915
its one-image-per-class results match what prior
methods needed ten images per class to achieve,

00:04:41.995 --> 00:04:45.993
using only about ten percent of their storage.

00:04:46.073 --> 00:04:50.072
It also stays best at every step of continual learning,

00:04:50.152 --> 00:04:54.150
and under corruption on ResNet18 it nearly doubles
the accuracy of prior methods.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:58.815
The ablations show sparsification is essentially free.

00:04:58.895 --> 00:05:03.181
Pruning each sparse coding matrix to just
forty-eight non-zero elements,

00:05:03.261 --> 00:05:07.546
about three hundred thousand parameters,

00:05:07.626 --> 00:05:11.912
meets the storage budget while keeping ConvNet
accuracy at seventy-three point five percent,

00:05:11.992 --> 00:05:16.277
barely below the seventy-four percent of the full
fifteen-million-parameter model.

00:05:16.357 --> 00:05:20.643
Push k too low, down to twelve, and accuracy collapses,

00:05:20.723 --> 00:05:25.008
so a moderate value is best and even improves
cross-architecture generalization.

00:05:25.088 --> 00:05:29.374
On the network side, two recurrent blocks and three
heads work best.

00:05:29.454 --> 00:05:33.739
And qualitatively,

00:05:33.819 --> 00:05:38.105
synthetic images before and after sparsification to
zero point three percent density look almost identical,

00:05:38.185 --> 00:05:42.470
confirming that the pruned features preserve the
global semantics.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.994
The lasting message of SPEED is that
parameterization deserves as much attention as the matching objective.

00:05:47.074 --> 00:05:51.218
By treating a synthetic dataset as a shared
dictionary of spatial-agnostic epitomic tokens,

00:05:51.298 --> 00:05:55.442
sparse per-image coding matrices,

00:05:55.522 --> 00:05:59.666
and a small recurrent network that reassembles them,

00:05:59.746 --> 00:06:03.890
SPEED removes the spatial redundancy that naive
methods leave on the table.

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:08.114
The payoff is state-of-the-art distillation at a
fraction of the storage,

00:06:08.194 --> 00:06:12.338
biggest on high-resolution images,

00:06:12.418 --> 00:06:16.562
plus better generalization to unseen architectures
and stronger robustness to corruption.

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:20.786
In short, sparse,

00:06:20.866 --> 00:06:25.010
shared representation is a powerful and general
lever for making tiny synthetic datasets do far more.
